Java

Unit.java — the enum is similar to the JS lookup tables. Instead of UNIT_CATEGORY and unit_label, Java bundles the same info into one enum where each constant carries its own label and category as fields

ConversionEngine.convert - same as JS - from Kelvin to unit conversion. You can't pass a Unit that isn't one of the ten declared constants, because the compiler won't let you

Grader.isNumeric The try/catch exists because Double.parseDouble is Java's equivalent of JS's Number(...) - unlike JS's Number("dogcow") quietly returns NaN and keep going, Java's Double.parseDoube("dogcow") throws a NumberFormatException instead of returning anything.
So the try/catch is doing the job that Number.isFinite does implicitly in JS — catching the "this wasn't a number at all" case.

